\section{FDR Sensitivity Analysis}
\label{sec:fdr_sensitivity}

Table~\ref{tab:fdr_sensitivity} reports the PermG forward significance counts under three correction standards. The consensus threshold $K{\geq}4/5$ used in the main text (Table~\ref{tab:generalization}) relies on raw permutation $p{<}0.05$, with cross-method agreement serving as multiplicity control.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{PermG forward significance under different multiple testing corrections. Raw: permutation $p{<}0.05$. Per-method BH: Benjamini--Hochberg correction applied within each method's 8 tests. Pooled BH: correction across all 40 tests (8 pairs $\times$ 5 methods).}
\label{tab:fdr_sensitivity}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Setting & Raw & Per-method BH & Pooled BH \\
\midrule
Baseline (Pythia-410M) & 6/8 & 6/8 & 6/8 \\
Architecture (OPT-1.3B) & 6/8 & 6/8 & 6/8 \\
Domain (C4) & 8/8 & 8/8 & 8/8 \\
Scale (Pythia-1.4B) & 5/8 & 2/8 & 3/8 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

C4 and OPT-1.3B PermG effects are strong enough that all three correction standards produce identical significance counts, confirming that these results are not artifacts of uncorrected multiplicity. Pythia-1.4B PermG significance drops to 2--3/8 under BH correction because borderline token\_entropy $p$-values (0.027--0.053) are absorbed by the adjustment; however, threshold onset detection remains 8/8 in the same setting, confirming that temporal precedence holds across all pairs and that the PermG reduction reflects a power limitation at $T{=}11$ time points rather than absence of the underlying signal.
